From d8344ef35c5880288ff32455d48913227a8e695c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 24bit192khz <118996408+24bit192kHz@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 19:53:23 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] mb/lenovo/sklkbl_thinkpad: Power the dGPU in the
 bootblock

The dGPU sits behind PCIe root port 1, and FSP-S disables root ports
that have no link. dgpu_detect() powered the GPU in the ramstage, after
FspSiliconInit, when root port 1 was gone already. The GPU could only
show up after a warm reset, as its pads keep their state, which matches
the comment about the sequence working "sometimes".

Power the GPU and release its reset in the bootblock. Root port 1 uses
CLKREQ0#, and that pad has to be in its native function for the PCH to
drive the reference clock, so configure it there, too. Wait for power
good with a timeout instead of forever; on the T480 it takes 4.7 ms.

FSP-S disables the root port for good: on a warm reset it does not find
the GPU, even if it has power and left reset long before. This is what
happens on the first boot after the GPU was enabled. A reset with a
power cycle enables the root port again, so do a full reset in that
case. MEM_SR in GEN_PMCON_A is only set after a reset without power
cycle, so there is at most one such reset. If there is still no link,
power the GPU off.

The power and reset pads are in the RSMRST well and keep their state in
S3 to S5. Remove them from the GPIO tables of the ramstage, which would
turn the GPU off before FSP-S, and turn the GPU off in the SMI handler
before the system goes to sleep.

TEST=Lenovo ThinkPad T480 with GeForce MX150 (10de:1d10). With the dGPU
     enabled it is at 01:00.0 with an 8 GT/s x4 link after a cold boot,
     a warm reset and S3 resume. The first boot after enabling it does
     one full reset. With the dGPU disabled the power rail stays off.
     T480s and T580 are build tested only.

